                <pb facs="0502" n="1336" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1336"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 659.119/410</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Denmark</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grant-Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-08-01T14:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 1, 1918, 2 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 2, 2.16 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2571. My 2564, July 30, 8 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d416-f1"/> [I understand]
                    that as a result of a conference yesterday attended by the Prime Minister, the
                    Minister of Foreign Affairs, representatives of the associations and of
                    agriculture, and Mr. H. N. Andersen for shipping,
                    telegraphic instructions were sent last night to the Danish delegates now at
                    Washington to offer further concessions as to tonnage and the reduction of
                    exporting to Germany. Mr. Andersen read me the telegram
                    which confers broader powers on the delegates, authorizes reopening the
                    negotiations on these lines, and urges them to establish mutual confidence. The
                    desire to reach a speedy agreement appears so keen that it seems probable the
                    proposals might be entirely recast to suit the Allied interest. Copy to
                    London.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grant-Smith</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
